As a promising additive manufacturing (AM) technology, the applications of selective laser melting (SLM) are expanding. Yet, due to the complex structure of SLM machines and low processing rates, the SLM process is highly energy-intensive. Energy forecasting is crucial for accurate evaluation and reduction of SLM energy consumption. However, due to the diversity of SLM machines and their various operating states, the energy consumption of SLM processes is difﬁcult to predict. This article presents a novel method to forecast the energy consumption of SLM processes. The proposed approach is based on the power modelling of machine subsystems and the temporal modelling of sub-processes. Through identifying the working statuses of subsystems of SLM machines in each sub-process, forecast accuracy can be greatly improved. Two cases of aluminium components fabricated by an SLM process using an SLM 280HL facility are selected to demonstrate the effectiveness of the proposed method. Results show that the proposed method outperforms speciﬁc, stage-based and subsystem-based energy benchmark models in energy consumption forecasting.
Keywords: 3D printing; additive manufacturing; energy consumption; selective laser melting; energy saving
1. Introduction The industrial sector accounts for almost one third (31.8%) of the global energy consumption (EIA 2016) and imposes tremendous environmental burdens. Lots of attention has been drawn to address the environmental concerns of the industry, including life cycle management of products (Gu et al. 2019; Lv et al. 2019), environmental impact assessment of equipment (Zhang et al. 2018), wastewater discharge (Sun, Xu, et al. 2019), air emissions (Sun, Zhou, et al. 2019), solid waste recycling (Zhao et al. 2018), energy benchmark of machining systems (Cai, Li, et al. 2020), energy performance certiﬁcation (Cai, Liu, et al. 2019) and waste heat recovery (Sun, Yue, et al. 2018; Zhang et al. 2016). Energy consumption prediction and conservation are hot topics in the current research (Sun, Wang, et al. 2018). However, research on energy forecasting and energy saving of emerging technologies is still lacking.
Additive Manufacturing (AM), also known as three-dimensional (3D) printing, is a technique to produce a 3D part from a digital model layer upon layer under computer control (Huang et al. 2013). AM is particularly applicable in producing highly-complex and lightweight parts, which are widely used in gas turbine, aircraft, medical implants and so on (Li et al. 2019). The global market share of AM is growing rapidly from $5 billion in 2016 to $12.08 billion in 2018, and expected to exceed $21 billion by 2020 (Attaran 2017). Compared to conventional manufacturing, AM could be more environmentally benign due to reduced material use and elimination of the need for moulds, dies, cutting tools and cutting ﬂuids (Walachowicz et al. 2017). However, the introduction of AM technology will place a new burden on the environment mainly due to the electricity usage (Faludi et al. 2017). Recent research has shown that the values of speciﬁc energy consumption (SEC) for AM processes are 1–2 orders of magnitude higher than those for conventional manufacturing processes, such as casting, machining, or injection moulding (Kellens, Baumers, et al. 2017). It can be expected that intensive electricity consumption will become a constraint for the large-scale application of AM technology in the future considering the energy cost and environmental impacts. Fortunately, there is enormous potential for energy savings of AM processes through product and process design. For instance, studies have shown that at least 43% of energy consumption from AM processes could be saved through optimal parameter setting (Mognol, Lepicart, and Perry 2006). In order to quantify and reduce the energy consumed by AM processes, it is imperative to develop simple and accurate methods to forecast the
