energy consumption (Jia et al. 2018, 2019; He et al. 2019). Although some research has been done on the energy modelling of AM processes, it is still limited and insufﬁcient. The energy characteristics of AM machines and temporal characteristics of AM sub-processes are not completely understood, resulting in inevitable model discrepancies. Developing energy consumption models of AM processes, especially power and time models by taking into account the product design, machine settings and process parameters becomes a crucial research question to foster the sustainability of AM.
Amongst various AM processes, selective laser melting (SLM) is a burgeoning technology due to its ability to manufacture metal components with nearly any geometry from computer aided design (CAD) model (Yap et al. 2015; Olakanmi, Cochrane, and Dalgarno 2015). It creates 3D parts through the power of laser beam which is absorbed by the metal powders and resulting in the locally melting and fusing of the metallic particles (Olakanmi, Cochrane, and Dalgarno 2015). In this study, SLM is selected as a representative AM process for energy consumption prediction. An SLM process can be characterised by multiple stages (such as warming up, building and cooling down) and multiple energy consuming subsystems (such as computer and display, lasers, heating and cooling system). In addition, the subsystems exhibit various states (i.e. on, off or intermitted running) and lead to power variations during the manufacturing process, which increases the complexity of the energy prediction.
This study aims at developing a simple and accurate model to forecast the total energy consumption of the SLM processes by adding the energy consumption of each subsystem. First, the power consumption of each machine subsystem is modelled by taking into account its operating states and parameters. Then, the temporal model of each sub-process is established based on the product design parameters of volume and surface area and process parameters. Finally, the total energy consumption is calculated using matrix multiplication based on the previously developed power and time models. The proposed method could be used to accurately quantify the energy consumption and the models are readily to be integrated with product or process design software and used for developing sustainable manufacturing options prior to real manufacturing.
The remainder of the paper is organised as follows: the next section presents a review of the current study on the energy consumption measurement and modelling of AM processes. Section 3 describes the development of the energy consumption modelling methodology for SLM process. Two case studies for predicting the energy consumption of an SLM process are demonstrated in Section 4. Section 5 shows the comparison of the predicted and measured energy consumption for manufacturing the AlSi10Mg components using an SLM 280HL facility and compares the proposed and existing energy prediction models. Finally, the conclusions and future works are summarised in Section 6.

2. State-of-the-art
AM, which is believed to be a signiﬁcant technology in the fourth industrial revolution, has grown rapidly in the past 5 years (Attaran 2017). With the increased use of the AM technology, increasing concerns on environmental issues, especially on energy consumption, have been presented. Various research efforts have been conducted to evaluate and reduce the environmental impact of the AM processes, such as environmental impact analysis of AM (Faludi et al. 2015), environmental comparison of AM and conventional manufacturing (CM) (Walachowicz et al. 2017) and energy consumption and mechanical properties analysis of AM manufactured parts (Peng et al. 2018; Peng and Chen 2018; Zhu et al. 2019). In these studies, the energy consumption data is indispensable, which is usually acquired through measurement and energy consumption models. Based on the existing studies, the AM energy consumption models can be classiﬁed into three categories: speciﬁc energy models, stage-based energy models and subsystem-based energy models. Models that compute the energy demand by multiplying the SEC and the weight or volume of a part are called ‘speciﬁc energy models’. Here, the SEC refers to the energy consumed for each deposited kilogram or volume of material. Alternatively, models that predict the total energy consumption by calculating and adding the energy consumed in each stage are called ‘stage-based energy models’. Similarly, models that estimate the total energy by adding the energy consumed by each machine subsystem are called ‘subsystem-based energy models’.
The speciﬁc energy models are often used to estimate the energy consumption of various AM processes, such as electron beam melting (EBM) process for fabricating Ti-6Al-4V alloy parts (Priarone et al. 2017), SLM process for making aluminium alloys (Ingarao et al. 2018) and metal powder-based (AM) processes (Paul and Anand 2015; Watson and Taminger 2018) for making titanium alloys. In these studies, the SEC value is considered as a constant. However, it could be affected by the utilisation of the build volume, build density and orientation (Telenko and Seepersad 2012). In addition, it is highly dependent on the machine usage proﬁles and speciﬁc machine types (Kellens et al. 2011). Therefore, the speciﬁc energy models could only afford the rough estimation of AM energy consumption. For the application of stage-based energy models, the AM processes are divided into different stages, such as warming up and building stage for selective laser sintering (SLS) process (Telenko and Seepersad 2012), machine start-up and vacuum creation, platform preheating, parts building and machine cooling down stage for EBM process (Paris et al. 2016), and drying of printed layer, spreading of new layer, and printing of new layer for binder jetting (BJ) process (Xu et al. 2015). In these studies, the power and

