In addition to theoretical models, data-driven or statistical methods have been used to model the AM processes with the increasing use of artiﬁcial intelligence in AM. Statistical methods like deep learning (Zhang, Wang, and Gao 2019), random forests (Wu, Wei, and Terpenny 2019) and artiﬁcial neural networks (Wang et al. 2019) have been employed to reveal the inﬂuences of the process parameters on the tensile strength, surface roughness and surface defect of AM components. Recently, two research efforts have been performed aiming to address the energy consumption problems. One study is to extract geometry feature using machine learning method for energy consumption prediction in mask image projection stereolithography (Yang, He, and Li 2019). Another study is to establish energy consumption prediction model for AM system using clustering and deep learning techniques (Qin, Liu, and Grosvenor 2018). However, this approach is restricted to the same product design because of a lack of physical insights.
Although some preliminary research has been done, it cannot meet the need for the development of a comprehensive modelling methodology to forecast the energy consumption of an AM process before real manufacturing. Such models are urgent as the AM process consists of various sub-processes, during which the machine subsystems show high variance in their power proﬁles and activation time. To address this issue, this paper proposes a comprehensive method integrating power consumption modelling of machine subsystems and temporal modelling of each sub-process for energy consumption prediction of SLM processes. A state matrix is used to represent the working status of machine subsystems during each sub-process, which can help to capture the power variations during each sub-process. This approach enables fast, accurate prediction of energy consumption with different machine settings and process parameters. The proposed models are readily to be integrated with product or process design software and used for developing sustainable manufacturing options before real manufacturing.

