3.3. Working status of different subsystem
The energy consuming subsystems could be on, off or intermittently running during different sub-processes of the SLM process. It is critical to identify the subsystem working status for energy modelling. Here, the working status coefﬁcient kij is deﬁned as the ratio of running time and the total time spent by subsystem i in sub-process j. The working status of different subsystems during the SLM process is shown in Figure 2. The basic subsystem and water circulation unit run all the time, while the laser, recoater motor, electric valves and gas circulation pump motor only run at a certain stage of the SLM process. In these cases, the working status coefﬁcient kij = 1 or 0. For the platform heater, it is working continuously at its maximum capacity during the warming up stage. When the substrate of the platform is heated to the speciﬁed temperature, the heater works intermittently to keep the substrate at that temperature. The coefﬁcient kij is calculated as:

kij = tho/(tho + thof) = Phb/Ph

(11)

where tho and thof are the running time and idle time of the heater, Ph is the heating capacity of the heater, Phb is the average power consumed by the heater to maintain the platform temperature during the building stage. For water-cooling unit, two states can be observed: standby mode and working mode (Le Bourhis et al. 2013). These two modes appear and disappear alternately. The coefﬁcient kij can be considered as the ratio of the runtime to the cycle time:

kij = tcw/(tcw + tcs)

(12)

where tcw and tcs are the time spent of the water-cooling unit in the working mode and standby mode during one cycle. The powder vibrating sieve is controlled manually by the operator and its working status is excluded from this study.
Then the energy consumption of the SLM will be calculated using Equation (1) with the obtained power and working status of different components as well as the time spent during each sub-process.

4. Case study
Two case studies were performed in Xi’an Ruite 3D Technology Company to validate the proposed method. A group of components were used as the ﬁrst case study (case 1). There are 17 components of different shapes and heights and both full dense parts and support structures are fabricated in this case study. The other case study (case 2) employs three components with internal cooling channels. The parts in both case studies were fabricated using aluminium AlSi10Mg powder on an SLM 280HL facility, SLM Solutions GmbH, Germany (see Figure 3). The machine is equipped with two 400 W ﬁbre lasers with a focal laser beam diameter of 80 µm. During the fabricating process, two lasers are working together, and the product design and process parameters are shown in Table 1. The parts were fabricated at an average room temperature of 27.0°C and 5°C for case 1 and case 2, respectively. The part fabrication was conducted in an inert gas atmosphere. Before the building process began, the oxygen level was reduced from 21% in air to below 0.1% to prevent oxidation and reactions of the AlSi10Mg powder. The platform was heated up from 27.0°C and 48.5°C to 150°C to reduce the thermal induced internal stress of the manufactured parts for case 1 and case 2, respectively. During the manufacturing process, the time required for recoating one layer was 11 s (unidirectional recoating) and 8 s (bi-directional recoating) for case 1 and case 2, respectively. After the end of the building process, the platform was

