The energy consumption during the manufacturing process of the selected aluminium components was measured for the validation of the proposed method by an in-house developed power acquisition device. The device consists of an NI data acquisition chassis (Cdaq-9174), two data acquisition cards (NI-9215), three voltage sensors (LEM LV25-P) and three current sensors (LEM HAS 50-S) (see Figure 4(b)). The sensors were mounted on the wire in the electrical cabinet of the SLM machine. The data acquisition frequency is 5000 Hz and the power data were recorded every 0.1 s.
The input data for the proposed method include power and time data, which are used to get the power and time consumption models for predicting the energy consumption of the SLM process. A test procedure is designed to measure the power data of the machine subsystems. First, the SLM machine was started up and the power demand of basic subsystems was measured in idle mode. Then, the power consumed by the remaining subsystems was measured. Each subsystem was activated for three times, and the machine dwelled in each state for at least 10 s to achieve statistically signiﬁcant data. The heater, water circulation unit, water cooling unit, laser, recoater motor, electric valves, gas circulation pump motor and powder vibrating sieving machine were switched on and off one after another. The laser was controlled to operate with different output power levels (0–400 W) and the recoater motor was controlled to move the recoater back and forth. To obtain the input time data, oxygen concentration and the time spent are recorded from the software interface of the SLM machine during the inert gas ﬂooding process. Similarly, temperature of the platform and the time spent are recorded during the preheating and the cooling down processes to obtain the time-temperature curves.

