5. Results and discussion
5.1. Measured and predicted energy consumption
The power and time consumption during the SLM process were measured, as shown in Figure 9. The total time consumed is 16 h 27 min and 3 h 21 min, during which the machine consumed 50.79 and 8.87 kWh for case 1 and case 2, respectively. The power ﬂuctuates due to the intermittent operation of water-cooling system and lasers. The building phase dominates the energy consumption of the SLM process, accounting for 91.79% and 57.53% of total energy use, followed by the cooling down phase at 5.13% and 23.19%, and the warming up phase at 3.08% and 19.28% for case 1 and case 2, respectively.
The results of the experiments to manufacture the part shown in Figure 3 using process parameters in Table 1 are summarised in Table 3. The predicted times in the warming up and cooling down phases for case 1 are very close to the measured times. For case 2, the measured time is longer than the predicted time in the warming up and shorter than the predicted time in the cooling down phase due to low ambient temperature of 5°C. For both cases, the measured time in the building process is over 20% longer than the predicted time. This could be explained by that the laser jump time being not included in the prediction model and the unsynchronised operating of the two lasers. For the scanning of one layer, one laser usually works longer than the other. The predicted total energy consumption is lower than the measured one, which could be explained by the predicted time being less than the actual time consumption. For case 1, the relative errors for predicting the energy consumption during the warming up and cooling down phases fall within 5%, and become 16.8% for energy prediction of building phase. For case 2, these prediction errors range between 13.28% and 20.54% for all phases due to the high time prediction errors (more than 13%). The errors for predicting the total energy consumption are smaller (15.59%

